   "source": [
    "## 6. Rank analysis of the pooled covariances\n",
    "\n",
    "Stage 1's open question is whether a **basis-aware** or **allocation-aware** method can recover the partial-metric oracle's win without the non-orthogonal scaling that breaks the V3 backend. The rank structure of the pooled Q and K covariances is the first evidence on whether that program has headroom at all.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Note on layer 0 — excluded from the plots below\n",
    "\n",
    "All rank and alignment plots in this section **skip layer 0**. Layer 0 behaves qualitatively differently from the rest of the stack in three concrete ways, and mixing it into aggregate plots obscures the behavior of the other 35 layers:\n",
    "\n",
    "- its `k_post` covariance is effectively rank-one (Shannon rank ≈ 3 out of 128) — nearly all of K's energy lives in a single direction;\n",
    "- its Q and K top-32 subspaces are **adversarially misaligned** (alignment factor ≈ 0.06× vs. a random-subspace baseline of 1.0×) — Q's dominant directions actively avoid K's variance;\n",
    "- it dominated the global averages in the Stage 1 oracle study ([stage1_experiments_and_findings](/vault/amir/efficient-llm/teamily-project/notes/stage1/stage1_experiments_and_findings.md:1)), driving apparent oracle wins that reversed once layer 0 was excluded.\n",
    "\n",
    "Early / embedding-adjacent transformer layers diverging from the rest of the stack is a repeatedly-observed phenomenon in the KV-cache compression and transformer-interpretability literature — the first layer (and, relatedly, \"attention sink\" tokens) routinely needs special treatment or higher precision in KV-quantization work.\n",
    "\n",
    "The utility cell below sets `START_LAYER = 1`; change it to `0` to include layer 0 again.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Q spectrum — the headroom bound.** If $\\mathrm{Cov}(q)$ is nearly isotropic (effective rank ≈ $d_h = 128$), then $M_q \\approx c \\cdot I$ and the optimal geometry-aware quantizer reduces to standard MSE quantization — no geometry to inject. Low Q rank means queries are concentrated in a subspace and geometry-aware quantization can redirect bits into that subspace.\n",
    "- **K spectrum — the baseline compressibility.** If K already sits in a small subspace, the V3 baseline's implicit handling (unit-normalize + random rotation + scalar quantize) does much of the work on its own, and the marginal gain from Q-aware geometry will be smaller.\n",
    "\n",
    "All measures below are computed from `pooled[name][1]` — the **centered** covariance at each `(layer, head)`, averaged over the `10128` prefill tokens pooled across the three LongBench-E tasks in the `small` bundle.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Three \"soft\" rank measures\n",
    "\n",
    "Each is a function of the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 \\ge \\cdots \\ge \\lambda_d \\ge 0$ of $\\mathrm{Cov}(x)$.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Shannon rank:** $\\exp\\!\\bigl(-\\sum_i p_i \\log p_i\\bigr)$ with $p_i = \\lambda_i / \\sum_j \\lambda_j$. Exponential of spectral entropy. Equal $\\lambda$ → $d$. One dominant $\\lambda$ → $1$. Most sensitive to tail shape.\n",
    "- **Stable rank:** $(\\sum_i \\lambda_i) / \\lambda_{\\max} = \\mathrm{tr}(\\Sigma) / \\lambda_{\\max}$. Frobenius²-to-operator² ratio. Cheap, robust to tail noise, lower-bounded by $1$. Nearly blind to the tail — dominated by the top eigenvalue.\n",
    "- **Participation ratio:** $(\\sum_i \\lambda_i)^2 / \\sum_i \\lambda_i^2$. Sits between Shannon rank and stable rank in tail sensitivity.\n",
    "\n",
    "All three map \"equal eigenvalues\" → $d$ and \"one dominant eigenvalue\" → $1$, but they differ in how sharply they count tail directions.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Cumulative-variance ranks\n",
    "\n",
    "For bit-allocation questions it's useful to know how many eigendirections carry a given fraction of the variance:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\mathrm{rank\\_at}(p) \\;=\\; \\min \\Bigl\\{\\, r \\;:\\; \\tfrac{\\sum_{i=1}^{r} \\lambda_i}{\\sum_i \\lambda_i} \\ge p \\,\\Bigr\\}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We report $\\mathrm{rank\\_at}(0.5)$, $\\mathrm{rank\\_at}(0.9)$, $\\mathrm{rank\\_at}(0.99)$. These are the cleanest numbers for \"how many dimensions would you keep to capture $p$ of the energy.\""
   ]
  },
